@startuml TuiDesignLanguage
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
' purpose: the one design language every proveo terminal surface renders in, and why the status stream had to join it
' covers: internal/ui/**, internal/posture/posture.go, internal/choiceui/topology.go
'   the prompt that already obeyed it .. _spec/internal/choiceui/choice-prompt-render.puml
'   its glyph + width rules ............ _spec/internal/choiceui/topology-strip.puml
'   its region budget .................. _spec/internal/choiceui/viewport.puml
'   the stream that now obeys it ....... _spec/internal/ui/output-vocabulary.puml
'   the palette these roles come from .. _spec/_conventions/spec-conventions.puml
'   surfaces deliberately left out ..... _spec/_plans/tui-style-deferred-surfaces.puml
'
' ── WHY THIS FILE EXISTS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
' proveo had TWO terminal designs. internal/choiceui — the full-screen prompt —
' paints through a five-role palette, measures every rune it writes, wraps and
' marks its own truncation, and drops a whole region rather than clipping one.
' internal/ui — the streaming status output, which is MOST of what an operator
' reads — emitted no colour whatsoever and decorated each line with an emoji
' chosen at the call site: 23 distinct glyphs over 57 sites.
'
' The two sat side by side in one run. The prompt looked designed; the prelude
' under it looked like debug logging that had been decorated after the fact.
' This file is the single language, and the stream is now one of its speakers
' rather than a second design.
'
' ── ROLE IS A CLOSED SET, NOT A PICTURE PER LINE ───────────────────────────
' A call site names WHAT A LINE IS ABOUT and the language decides how that
' looks. It never chooses a glyph. That is the whole difference between the two
' halves as they were: choiceui has ONE glyphSet whose every rune is declared in
' one struct, so a new element cannot invent a rune; ui.Iconf took the rune as
' an ARGUMENT, so all 57 sites were free to, and did.
'
' The six roles are the identity's own — the same names, the same colours the
' diagrams in this tree are drawn with, so a component that is <<host>> on a
' diagram prints host-cyan in a terminal. Two of the six carry no verb yet
' (cloud has three sites, async has one); the role exists because the identity
' has six, and a role with no caller is documented rather than invented.
'
' ── SEVERITY IS A GLYPH, ROLE IS A COLOUR ─────────────────────────────────
' Both, so neither is load-bearing alone. spec-conventions requires edge intent
' to survive greyscale; the same applies here. A status line read with NO_COLOUR
' still distinguishes ok from warn from failure by its mark, and a line read at
' a glance in colour still says which layer it came from without being parsed.
'
' Severity has a colour too, and it is the identity's own pair: $SUCCESS #009532
' and $ERROR #CB2000, the green and red proveo.puml already exposes as
' successLabel() and errorLabel(). internal/ui had never declared the green — it
' mirrored the six ROLE colours and stopped — so the ok mark was painted teal
' with ColorApp for want of one. That absence is precisely what pulls a codebase
' towards U+2705 and U+274C: an emoji that is already green, already red, and
' already the wrong width. Declaring the colour removes the reason to reach.
'
' So: a plain text glyph, painted. U+2713 in success green, U+26A0 in attention
' lime, U+00D7 in error red. Nothing in the mark table carries its own colour,
' which is the same rule as "no emoji" seen from the other side.
'
' ── NO EMOJI, AND THE REASON IS WIDTH — NOT TASTE ─────────────────────────
' topology-strip already banned them from the figure. The ban generalises here
' unchanged: an emoji is one to two columns depending on the font, and three of
' the glyphs in use carried U+FE0F, the variation selector that go-runewidth
' measures as ONE column and every terminal draws as TWO. A stream that cannot
' predict its own column count cannot wrap, and the screenshot that prompted
' this file proves it — every long line broke mid-word ("limitin/g it",
' "l/oopback") because nothing measured anything.
'
' Box-drawing and geometric glyphs stay in. They are ambiguous-width too, but
' they are ASKED rather than assumed — measured the way the writer will paint
' them — and they are the BANNER's vocabulary: dots interrupting rules. The
' figure already draws the run that way; the stream now marks its lines that way.
'
' ── THE STREAM IS GROUPED THE WAY THE FORM IS ─────────────────────────────
' A divider names the concern the lines under it belong to, and the names are the
' CHOICE FORM's own wherever the form has one: egress and credentials are its
' rows, execution and interface its two plane dividers. An operator answers a
' question called "egress" and then reads what happened under a heading called
' "egress". A parallel vocabulary — "network", "creds" — would make the two
' surfaces describe one run in two languages.
'
' THE HEADING IS LAZY, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE MECHANISM. Nearly every line in a
' prelude is conditional: no gh session, no symlinks, no broker, no sub-scope.
' A stage that declared a section and then found nothing to say would leave a
' heading with nothing under it. So declaring is cheap and DRAWING is deferred to
' the first line that actually arrives, which means no caller ever has to ask
' whether it is about to print something. Re-declaring the heading already on
' screen is a no-op, so a stage may announce itself without checking what the
' previous one did — and that is what lets a later stage CONTINUE a block rather
' than reopen it.
'
' ── TWO PASSES, BECAUSE A RUN HAS TWO MOMENTS ─────────────────────────────
' A run reports what it resolved, then starts. Three concerns appear in both
' halves — credentials are read and then injected, execution is chosen and then
' begun — and reusing one name would make the second heading read as a repeat
' rather than as a different moment. So the live half has its own names:
'
'   resolved ... scope · run · credentials · workspace · egress · execution
'   live ....... starting · secrets · interface · results
'
' Every divider in a run therefore appears exactly ONCE. Getting there took one
' reordering, in run.selectBackend: the sbx baseline warning and the .env warning
' now print before the "backend:" line, so they land in the egress block the
' broker line opened instead of splitting execution in two. Deciding was
' separated from reporting to make that safe — nothing about which backend runs
' moved, only the order of two report calls.
'
' ── THE DIVIDER IS CHOICEUI'S, RE-ANCHORED ────────────────────────────────
' Same construction and same secondary colour: rules, the label spaced, rules.
' choiceui CENTRES its heading on 72 columns and is right to — the rows beneath
' it are a column grid, so a centred label sits over the middle of the options it
' names. This stream is left-aligned prose, and centring here floated the label
' at column 32 above text starting at column 2, reading as a caption for nothing.
' So it is anchored at the text column with the trailing run out to the MEASURED
' width. An unmeasurable writer — a log, a pipe, CI — gets choiceui's short form
' instead, because a hundred dashes per section is noise in a transcript and the
' width would be a constant pretending to be a measurement.
'
' ── THE TIER IS ONE TYPE NOW ──────────────────────────────────────────────
' Three packages had their own idea of how decorated a terminal is:
' posture.GlyphMode (three values, read from PROVEO_GLYPHS), choiceui.GlyphTier
' (two), and nothing at all in ui, which had only a boolean Plain. A design
' language with three notions of its own tier is not one language, so ui owns
' the type and the other two alias it. run/topology.go's stripGlyphs — the
' function that translated between two of them — is gone.

title TUI design language — one palette, one closed glyph set, two speakers

package "the language — internal/ui" as LANG <<app>> {
  component "Role\nhost · app · async\ncloud · store · error" as ROLE <<app>>
  component "GlyphTier\nNerd · ASCII · Off\nPROVEO_GLYPHS, once per session" as TIER <<host>>
  component "marks[tier][severity]\nok · warn · fail · dot" as MARK <<app>>
  component "palette\nColorHost · ColorApp · ColorAsync\nColorCloud · ColorDB · ColorError" as PAL <<db>>
  component "wrap · clip · textWidth\nmeasured, hanging indent" as FIT <<app>>
  component "Section*\nten names, closed\nlazy heading" as SEC <<app>>
}

component "internal/choiceui\nfull-screen prompt\nglyphSet · pen · canvas · ladder" as CUI <<app>>
component "internal/ui Printer\nstreaming status\nHostf · Appf · Storef · Cloudf\nOkf · Warnf · Failf · Notef\nSection(ui.Section*)" as PRN <<app>>
component "internal/posture\nGlyphMode = ui.GlyphTier\nlsp: row devicons" as POST <<app>>

database "terminal\nrole colour + tiered mark\nwrapped to width" as TERM <<db>>
database "run log · pipe · CI\nok: · warn: · error:\nno colour, no glyph" as LOG <<db>>

component "emoji\n23 glyphs, 57 call sites\n3 carrying U+FE0F" as EMOJI <<error>>

ROLE ARROW_MAIN PAL : colour
ROLE ARROW_MAIN MARK : dot
TIER ARROW_MAIN MARK : which rune
CUI ARROW_MAIN ROLE
PRN ARROW_MAIN ROLE
POST ARROW_MAIN TIER
PRN ARROW_MAIN SEC : declare, draw on first line
SEC ARROW_MAIN FIT : rule runs to width
CUI ARROW_MAIN SEC : the label vocabulary
PRN ARROW_MAIN FIT : measure, then wrap
CUI ARROW_MAIN FIT
PRN ARROW_MAIN TERM : fancy
PRN ARROW_OPTIONAL LOG : plain — a degradation
MARK ARROW_ERROR EMOJI : withdrawn

note right of ROLE
  **Six roles, and a call site picks one — never a rune.**
  ""ui\.Appf("image: %s", img)"" says what the line is
  about; the language decides that this is teal with a
  ""●"" in front of it. ""Iconf(icon, …)"" — which took the
  decoration as an argument — is gone, and with it the
  23-glyph vocabulary no single file ever held.
end note

note bottom of MARK
  **Severity is the mark, role is the colour.**

  |= |= nerd |= ascii |= plain |= colour |
  | ok   | ""✓"" | ""+"" | ""ok: ""    | <color:#009532>#009532 green</color> |
  | warn | ""⚠"" | ""!"" | ""warn: ""  | <color:#818D00>#CBDB2A lime</color> |
  | fail | ""×"" | ""x"" | ""error: "" | <color:#CB2000>#CB2000 red</color> |
  | role | ""●"" | ""o"" | (none)  | the role's own |
  | note | (two spaces — a continuation) |||

  Every mark is ONE column plus one space, so the text
  column is the same on every line whatever the tier,
  and a wrapped line's hanging indent lands under it.
end note

note bottom of TIER
  **The tier is a capability of the session, so it is read
  once and never re-decided.** A terminal offers no way to
  ask whether its font carries a codepoint, so this is the
  operator's declaration — ""PROVEO_GLYPHS=ascii"" — not a
  probe. ""off"" strips the marks and keeps the colour.

  ""Off"" used to reach ""choiceui\.glyphsFor"" and come back
  with the NERD set, because the switch tested for ASCII
  rather than against Nerd. Aliasing the three types onto
  one made that reachable, so it is fixed here.
end note

note right of PRN
  **Plain stays greppable, and that is why it is not
  a second design.** No TTY, ""NO_COLOR"", ""TERM=dumb"",
  or the tee's copy on its way to the run log: a SEVERITY
  mark becomes a word and the colour is dropped, so
  ""grep -c 'warn:'"" over a transcript counts what a
  human saw on screen. A role's dot is dropped rather
  than worded — ""host: image: …"" reads worse than the
  fact alone, and a layer is not an event to grep for. ""Notef"" carries no marker in either
  rendering — it is the continuation under a preceding
  verb, indented to that verb's text column.
end note

note right of SEC
  **Ten names, and a call site may not invent one.** Six
  come from the form — ""egress"", ""credentials"",
  ""execution"", ""interface"" are its rows and dividers —
  so a run is narrated under the headings the operator
  was asked about. The other four name the live phase,
  which the form has no row for.

  Lazy, because nearly every prelude line is
  conditional. Declaring costs nothing; the first line
  that follows draws the heading. A stage that reports
  nothing leaves no divider, and re-declaring the
  current heading continues its block rather than
  reopening it.

  A contract test refuses a literal argument and fails
  on a constant no call site names — a heading that can
  never appear is as much a defect as a missing one.
end note

note bottom of FIT
  **A stream that cannot measure itself cannot wrap.**
  Width comes from the writer's own ioctl, once per line,
  and falls back to 80 for anything that is not a
  terminal. Continuations are indented to the text column,
  never to zero, so one status line reads as one thing.
  Truncation — where a line must be one row — is MARKED
  with ""…"", exactly as ""choiceui\.clip"" marks it.
end note

note right of EMOJI
  **The ban is width, not taste.** Three of the marks in
  use carried U+FE0F. ""go-runewidth"" reports the variation
  selector as one column; terminals draw the pair as two.
  Four sites had even written theirs as a Go escape,
  ""\\U0001f5c2"", which no reader can see.

  The tempting ones are U+2705 and U+274C — a green tick
  and a red cross with no selector to give them away.
  They are refused for the same reason and replaced the
  same way: a text glyph in ""ColorSuccess"" or
  ""ColorFailure"". A rune that carries its own colour is
  buying with columns what the palette gives away.

  A contract test parses every print site AND every
  comment, keyed on Emoji_Presentation rather than a
  hand-picked list, so the vocabulary cannot quietly
  reopen — and the codepoint is always nameable, so no
  comment needs the glyph to make its point.
end note

@enduml
